  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7004_003",
    "query": "Can a navigation app require a user to consent to selling their location data to third-party data brokers in order to use the app's basic GPS mapping features?",
    "target_section": "7004",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": false,
    "hit_in_top_1": false,
    "precision_at_k": 0.0,
    "key_term_recall": 0.2,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7004(a)(4)(B), bundling choices so that a consumer must consent to incompatible secondary uses (e.g., selling geolocation to data brokers) together with necessary operational uses (e.g., providing location-based navigation) is a prohibited choice architecture. The business must provide a separate, unbundled option for consent to incompatible secondary uses.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, the documents do not explicitly address the legality of \"pay-for-privacy\" models or the specific requirement to consent to the sale of data in exchange for the use of a navigation app's basic features.\n\nHowever, the provided text does outline the following relevant requirements regarding financial incentives and opt-out preference signals:\n\n*   **Financial Incentive Programs**: Under **\u00a7 7025(b)(4)**, if a business operates a financial incentive program that requires a consumer to consent to the sale or sharing of personal information, it must manage conflicts between that program and opt-out preference signals. If a user signals an intent to opt-out, the business may notify the consumer that doing so would withdraw them from the financial incentive program and ask them to affirm their intent to withdraw. \n*   **Opt-Out Rights**: **\u00a7 7025(b)(1)** states that a business must treat an opt-out preference signal as a valid request to opt-out of sale/sharing for that browser, device, and any associated consumer profile.\n*   **Contractual Requirements for Third Parties**: If a business shares personal information with a third party, it must enter into an agreement that identifies the limited and specified purpose for the data, requires the third party to comply with the CCPA, and grants the business the right to take steps to ensure the third party uses the data consistently with the business\u2019s obligations (**\u00a7 7053(a)(1)-(4)**).\n\nThe provided context does not contain information regarding whether a business is permitted to condition \"basic\" service functionality on the consent to sell personal information to data brokers. Therefore, this question cannot be fully answered with the provided source material.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.7567,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 34.097,
    "retrieved_sources": [
      {
