  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7220_006",
    "query": "Can an employer provide a single consolidated Pre-use Notice covering productivity software that monitors employees for work assignment, compensation, and demotion decisions?",
    "target_section": "7220",
    "expected_route": "HYBRID_BOTH",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": false,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 1.0,
    "key_term_recall": 0.5,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Yes. Under \u00a7 7220(e)(1), a business may provide a consolidated Pre-use Notice when using a single ADMT for multiple purposes. The regulation specifically highlights an employer using productivity monitoring software to determine work allocation/compensation and demotions as a valid consolidated notice use case, provided all required \u00a7 7220 disclosures are included for each purpose.",
    "generated_answer": "Yes, an employer may provide a consolidated Pre-use Notice for that scenario.\n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7220(e)(1)**, a business may provide a consolidated Pre-use Notice for the use of a single Automated Decision-Making Technology (ADMT) for multiple purposes. The regulation explicitly provides your scenario as an example:\n\n*   An employer may provide a consolidated Pre-use Notice to an employee that addresses the proposed use of productivity monitoring software to determine the employee\u2019s allocation/assignment of work, to determine compensation, and to determine which employees will be demoted (**\u00a7 7220(e)(1)**).\n\n**Compliance Requirements:**\nTo utilize a consolidated notice, the business must ensure that it includes all information required by Article 7220 for each of the proposed uses of the ADMT (**\u00a7 7220(e)**). This includes, but is not limited to:\n\n*   **Content Requirements**: The notice must provide a plain language explanation of the specific purposes, instructions on how to exercise opt-out and access rights (or appeal rights if an exception applies), a statement regarding non-retaliation, and detailed information on how the ADMT works and how decisions are made (**\u00a7 7220(c)**).\n*   **Formatting**: The notice must be presented prominently and conspicuously at or before the point of collection, or before processing if the information was previously collected for a different purpose (**\u00a7 7220(b)(2)**). \n*   **Trade Secret Exceptions**: Per **\u00a7 7220(d)**, the business is not required to include trade secrets (as defined in Civil Code section 3426.1) or information that would compromise the business\u2019s ability to prevent security incidents, resist illegal actions, or ensure physical safety.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9123,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 13.525,
    "retrieved_sources": [
