  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7152_002",
    "query": "What specific requirements apply to identifying categories of personal information and data minimization within a risk assessment report?",
    "target_section": "7152",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 1.0,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7152(a)(2), the risk assessment report must identify and document all categories of personal information to be processed (including sensitive personal information). Crucially, the assessment must document that the data collected constitutes the minimum personal information necessary to achieve the specific purpose of processing.",
    "generated_answer": "Under the California privacy regulations, specifically **\u00a7 7152(a)(2)**, a business must adhere to the following requirements regarding the identification of personal information and data minimization within a risk assessment report:\n\n*   **Identification of Categories**: The business must identify and document the specific categories of personal information to be processed. This must include any categories of sensitive personal information.\n*   **Data Minimization Requirement**: The documentation must explicitly include the **minimum personal information that is necessary** to achieve the business\u2019s identified purpose for processing that information. \n\nThese requirements ensure that the business does not process more personal information than is strictly required to fulfill the purpose documented under **\u00a7 7152(a)(1)**.\n\n### Related Considerations:\n*   **Material Changes**: Per **\u00a7 7155(a)(3)**, if a business makes a change to the \"minimum personal information necessary to achieve the purpose of the processing,\" this is considered a \"material change.\" In such cases, the business must update the risk assessment as soon as feasibly possible, but no later than 45 calendar days from the date of the change.\n*   **Comparable Processing**: Under **\u00a7 7156(a)**, a business may conduct a single risk assessment for a \"comparable set of processing activities\" (similar activities presenting similar risks), which may include documenting the categories of personal information for that set of activities.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8986,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 19.565,
    "retrieved_sources": [
