  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7025_002",
    "query": "If a consumer previously enabled an opt-out preference signal on their laptop, but later logs into their account from a smartphone that does NOT have the signal enabled, can the business treat the missing signal as consent to sell or share data?",
    "target_section": "7025",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.0,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7025(c)(5) and Example (C) in \u00a7 7025(c)(7), where a consumer is known to the business and has previously submitted an opt-out preference signal linked to their account, the business shall NOT interpret the absence of an opt-out preference signal on a different device or browser as consent to opt-in or sell personal information.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, **no**, the business may not treat the absence of the signal as consent to sell or share the consumer's personal information.\n\nThe requirements regarding this scenario are as follows:\n\n*   **Prohibition against interpreting the absence of a signal as consent:** Under **\u00a7 7025(b)(5)**, where a consumer is known to the business, the business shall not interpret the absence of an opt-out preference signal\u2014even after the consumer previously sent one\u2014as consent to opt-in to the sale or sharing of personal information.\n*   **Application to accounts:** When a consumer is known to a business (e.g., they are logged into an account), the business must treat a valid opt-out preference signal as a request to opt-out of sale/sharing for that browser/device *and* any consumer profile associated with that browser or device (**\u00a7 7025(b)(1)**).\n*   **Illustrative Example:** **\u00a7 7025(b)(7)(C)** specifically addresses this scenario:\n    *   If a consumer has enabled an opt-out preference signal while logged into their account, the business applies that request to the account.\n    *   If the consumer subsequently logs into that same account using a different device that does *not* have the signal enabled, the business **shall not interpret the absence of the opt-out preference signal as consent to opt-in to the sale of personal information.**",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9215,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 7.356,
