  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7001_019",
    "query": "Are the contents of a customer's emails or text messages always classified as sensitive personal information when processed by a business?",
    "target_section": "7001",
    "expected_route": "HYBRID_BOTH",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": false,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": false,
    "precision_at_k": 0.1667,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7001(bbb)(1)(E), the contents of a consumer's mail, email, and text messages are classified as sensitive personal information UNLESS the business is the intended recipient of the communication. When a consumer directly emails or texts a business, those contents are not classified as sensitive personal information under this subsection.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, the classification of the contents of emails or text messages as \"sensitive personal information\" depends on the business's relationship to those communications.\n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7001(bbb)(1)(E)**, sensitive personal information includes:\n\n*   \"The contents of a consumer\u2019s mail, email, and text messages **unless the business is the intended recipient of the communication.**\"\n\nTherefore, if a business is the intended recipient of the email or text message, those contents do not fall under the definition of sensitive personal information provided in **\u00a7 7001**. If the business is not the intended recipient\u2014for example, if they are intercepting or accessing communications between a consumer and a third party\u2014the contents are classified as sensitive personal information.",
