    "eval_id": "eval_7156_001",
    "query": "When is a business permitted to conduct a single CCPA risk assessment for multiple operational activities across different physical locations or marketing campaigns?",
    "target_section": "7156",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "HYBRID_BOTH",
    "route_correct": false,
    "route_reasoning": "Query contains ambiguous update keyword 'different'. Routing to hybrid policy + diff lookup.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.5,
    "key_term_recall": 0.25,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7156(a), a business may conduct a single risk assessment for a 'comparable set of processing activities' that present similar privacy risks to consumers\u2014for example, an in-store child birthday and holiday coupon mailing program across multiple retail stores that collects the same data using the same service providers and technology (\u00a7 7156(a)(1)).",
    "generated_answer": "Under the current regulatory framework, a business is permitted to conduct a single risk assessment for multiple operational activities provided they constitute a \u201ccomparable set of processing activities.\u201d\n\n### Requirements for a Single Risk Assessment\nAccording to **\u00a7 7156(a)**, a business may use a single risk assessment if the activities meet the following criteria:\n*   **Similarity**: The activities must be similar in nature.\n*   **Comparable Risk**: The activities must present similar risks to consumers\u2019 privacy.\n\nAs illustrated in the example regarding \"Business E\" in **\u00a7 7156(a)(1)**, if a business collects the same personal information in the same way for the same purpose across different locations or campaigns (such as multiple store mailings), it may consolidate these into one risk assessment. This is permissible even if the processing involves sensitive personal information, provided the underlying mechanics and privacy risk profiles are consistent.\n\n### Utilization of Existing Assessments\nAdditionally, **\u00a7 7156(b)** allows a business to leverage a risk assessment prepared for another purpose (such as compliance with another state's law) to satisfy the requirements of **\u00a7 7152**, provided that:\n*   The existing assessment contains all information required by **\u00a7 7152**; or \n*   It is paired with any \"outstanding information\" necessary to achieve full compliance with **\u00a7 7152**. \n\nIf the assessment from another jurisdiction lacks specific requirements\u2014such as those found in **\u00a7 7152(a)(2)-(3), (7)**, or the identification of the specific individual with the authority to decide on initiating the processing\u2014the business must supplement its documentation to include these mandatory details to meet CCPA requirements (**\u00a7 7156(b)(1)**).\n\n### Regulatory Context\nThese provisions for \"comparable sets of processing\" are part of the updated 2026 regulatory framework. Unlike previous iterations, **\u00a7 7156** provides explicit guidance on how to consolidate compliance documentation for efficient risk management. When determining if a risk assessment is required in the first instance, businesses must refer to **\u00a7 7150**, which mandates that assessments be performed before initiating any processing that presents a \"significant risk\" to consumer privacy.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8955,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 11.557,
