    "key_term_recall": 0.0,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7221(c)(4), a cookie banner or cookie control tool is explicitly prohibited from serving as an acceptable ADMT opt-out method because cookie tools pertain to data collection, not the automated processing of decisionmaking technology. ADMT opt-out methods must be specific to the right to opt-out of ADMT.",
    "generated_answer": "No, a business cannot rely solely on a standard website cookie banner or cookie management tool as an acceptable method for handling opt-out requests for Automated Decisionmaking Technology (ADMT).\n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7221(c)(4)**, a notification or tool regarding cookies (such as a cookie banner or cookie controls) is not, by itself, an acceptable method for submitting requests to opt-out of a business\u2019s use of ADMT. This is because cookie-related tools primarily concern the collection of personal information and do not necessarily address the specific use of ADMT.\n\nTo remain in compliance, the business must ensure the following:\n\n*   **Specific Purpose**: An acceptable method for submitting ADMT opt-out requests must be specific to the right to opt-out of the business\u2019s use of ADMT (**\u00a7 7221(c)(4)**).\n*   **Designated Methods**: A business must provide two or more designated methods for submitting these requests. For online interactions, at least one of these methods must be an interactive form accessible via an opt-out link provided in the Pre-use Notice. The link title must clearly state what the consumer is opting out of (e.g., \u201cOpt-out of Automated Decisionmaking Technology\u201d) (**\u00a7 7221(c)(1)**).\n*   **Ease of Use**: Regardless of the methods chosen, they must be easy for consumers to execute, require minimal steps, and comply with the requirements of **\u00a7 7004** (**\u00a7 7221(d)**).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9138,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 9.424,
