  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7302_002",
    "query": "Are CPPA probable cause proceedings open to the public by default, and how can an alleged violator request an in-person or public hearing?",
    "target_section": "7302",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.5,
    "key_term_recall": 0.0,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7302(c)(1), probable cause proceedings are closed to the public by default and conducted in whole or in part by telephone or videoconference. An alleged violator may make the proceeding public or in-person ONLY by filing a written request at least 10 business days prior to the proceeding.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, here are the requirements regarding the transparency and format of probable cause proceedings:\n\n### Public Access Status\n*   **Default Status**: Probable cause proceedings are **not** open to the public by default. According to **\u00a7 7302(c)(1)**, the proceeding shall be closed to the public.\n*   **Format**: The proceeding is conducted, in whole or in part, by telephone or videoconference by default (**\u00a7 7302(c)(1)**).\n\n### Requesting an In-Person or Public Proceeding\nIf an alleged violator wishes to change the default format or accessibility, they must follow these requirements:\n*   **Written Request**: The alleged violator must file a written request for an in-person or public proceeding (**\u00a7 7302(c)(1)**).\n*   **Deadline**: This request must be filed at least **10 business days** before the scheduled proceeding (**\u00a7 7302(c)(1)**).\n\n***\n\n**Note on Participation:** The proceeding is conducted informally, and the right to participate is limited exclusively to the alleged violator(s), their legal counsel, and the Enforcement Division (**\u00a7 7302(c)(2)**). If the alleged violator fails to attend, they waive their right to further probable cause proceedings under Civil Code section 1798.199.50 (**\u00a7 7302(c)(3)**).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8646,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 7.055,
    "retrieved_sources": [
