    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7155(a)(3), a change is material if it creates new negative impacts, increases the magnitude or likelihood of previously identified negative impacts (\u00a7 7152(a)(5)), or diminishes the effectiveness of safeguards (\u00a7 7152(a)(6)). Examples include changes to processing purpose, minimum data necessary, or spikes in privacy complaints. When a material change occurs, the business must update its risk assessment as soon as feasibly possible, but no later than 45 calendar days from the date of the material change.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the statutory comparative context provided, here is the analysis regarding \"material changes\" and risk assessment update timeframes:\n\n**1. Definition of \"Material Change\"**\nThe provided text contains only the definition of \"CCPA\" (formerly \u00a7 7001(g), now \u00a7 7001(h)). Because the provided context is limited strictly to this definition, there is **no information provided** regarding the definition of a \"material change\" to a processing activity.\n\n**2. Timeframe for Updating Risk Assessments**\nThe provided text does not contain any provisions regarding risk assessments or the timeframes required for updating them. Consequently, there is **no information provided** in the comparative context to answer this portion of your query.\n\n**3. Status of Provisions**\nAs the provided comparative context is limited exclusively to the definition of \"CCPA,\" it does not contain the sections of the regulations that govern risk assessments or material changes. Therefore, I cannot confirm whether those specific provisions have changed, remained the same, or been added between 2023 and 2026 based on the provided text. \n\nThe only change identified in the provided text is the relettering of the definition of \"CCPA\" from **\u00a7 7001(g)** in 2023 to **\u00a7 7001(h)** in 2026; the actual wording of the definition remains unchanged.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.7773,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
