  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7012_003",
    "query": "If a retail store or website allows a third party (such as a Wi-Fi provider or ad network) to collect personal data on its premises or site, who must provide the Notice at Collection?",
    "target_section": "7012",
    "expected_route": "HYBRID_BOTH",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": false,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.25,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7012(g)(1)\u2013(3), BOTH the first party and the third party controlling data collection have an obligation to provide a Notice at Collection. They may provide a single joint notice, or the third party must provide its own conspicuous notice (e.g., on its homepage, the Wi-Fi connection landing interface, or dashboard signage in rental vehicles).",
    "generated_answer": "Under the provided regulations, both the first party (the business operating the website or physical premises) and the third party (the entity controlling the collection) may have an obligation to provide a Notice at Collection.\n\n### General Requirements\n*   **Joint Responsibility:** When more than one business controls the collection of personal information, both the first party and the third party are required to provide a Notice at Collection. This applies to both websites and physical locations (\u00a7 7012(g)(1)).\n*   **Combined Notice:** To satisfy this requirement, the businesses may choose to provide a single Notice at Collection that encompasses their collective information practices (\u00a7 7012(g)(1)).\n*   **First Party Obligations:** The first party\u2019s obligation to provide a Notice at Collection is not affected by the third party's involvement, nor does it excuse the first party from complying with requests to opt-out of sale/sharing (\u00a7 7012(g)).\n\n### Specific Scenarios\nThe regulations provide specific guidance on how these notices must be delivered depending on the context:\n\n*   **Website Ad Networks:** If a first party allows a third-party ad network to collect information via its website, the first party may post a conspicuous link to its Notice at Collection on its homepage. The third-party ad network must either provide its own Notice at Collection on its own homepage or include its information practices within the first party\u2019s notice (\u00a7 7012(g)(3)(A)).\n*   **Physical Premises / Wi-Fi Services:** If a business (e.g., a coffee shop) allows a third party to collect information via Wi-Fi, the first party may post conspicuous signage at the entrance or point-of-sale directing consumers to the first party's online notice. The third party is required to post its own Notice at Collection on the interface or webpage the consumer sees before connecting to the service (\u00a7 7012(g)(3)(B)).\n*   **Retail/Physical Locations:** For third parties collecting information on another business\u2019s physical premises (e.g., in a retail store or vehicle), the third party must provide a Notice at Collection in a \"conspicuous manner\" at the location where the information is being collected (\u00a7 7012(g)(2)). For instance, in a vehicle, the third party might use signage on the dashboard directing consumers to an online notice (\u00a7 7012(g)(3)(C)).\n\n### Exemptions\n*   A business that does not collect or control the collection of personal information directly from the consumer is not required to provide a Notice at Collection, provided that the business also does not sell or share the consumer\u2019s personal information (\u00a7 7012(h)).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.88,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 10.248,
