  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7053_002",
    "query": "What contractual remediation rights must a first-party business hold against a third party that receives consumer data if an opt-out request is forwarded?",
    "target_section": "7053",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.1667,
    "key_term_recall": 0.5,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7053(a)(5), the contract must grant the business the right, upon notice, to take reasonable and appropriate steps to stop and remediate unauthorized use. For example, the business may require the third party to provide documentation verifying that it no longer retains or uses the personal information of consumers whose opt-out requests were forwarded by the first party.",
    "generated_answer": "Under the CCPA regulations, when a business sells or shares personal information with a third party and forwards an opt-out request, it must maintain specific contractual rights to ensure compliance and remediate unauthorized use.\n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7053(a)**, the agreement between the business and the third party must include the following provisions regarding remediation and enforcement:\n\n*   **Right to Ensure Compliance (\u00a7 7053(a)(4))**: The business must retain the right to take reasonable and appropriate steps to ensure that the third party uses personal information in a manner consistent with the business\u2019s obligations under the CCPA. This may include requiring the third party to attest that it treats the personal information in the same manner the business is obligated to treat it.\n*   **Right to Stop and Remediate Unauthorized Use (\u00a7 7053(a)(5))**: The business must have the right, upon notice, to take reasonable and appropriate steps to stop and remediate unauthorized use of personal information. Specifically regarding opt-out requests, the business may require the third party to provide documentation verifying that it no longer retains or uses the personal information of consumers who have submitted opt-out requests that were forwarded to them by the business.\n*   **Obligation to Notify of Non-Compliance (\u00a7 7053(a)(6))**: The contract must require the third party to notify the business if it makes a determination that it can no longer meet its obligations under the CCPA and its regulations.\n\n**Due Diligence Requirement:**\nPer **\u00a7 7053(b)**, whether a business exercises these contractual rights is a factor in determining whether the business has reason to believe a third party is violating the law. A business that fails to enforce the terms of its contract may be unable to rely on the defense that it did not have reason to believe the third party intended to violate the CCPA at the time of disclosure.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8869,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 8.893,
