    "hit_in_top_1": false,
    "precision_at_k": 0.4,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7123(e)(8), the cybersecurity audit report must include a statement signed and dated by the highest-ranking auditor certifying that they completed an independent review of the business's program and system, exercised objective and impartial judgment on all issues, and did not rely primarily on assertions or attestations by the business's management.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, a final cybersecurity audit report must include a specific signed and dated statement from the auditor, as well as a separate certification of completion submitted by the business to the Agency.\n\n### Auditor Sign-Off Requirements\nAccording to **\u00a7 7123(e)(8)**, the cybersecurity audit report must include a statement signed and dated by the highest-ranking auditor. This statement must certify that the auditor:\n*   Completed an independent review of the business\u2019s cybersecurity program and information system.\n*   Exercised objective and impartial judgment on all issues within the scope of the cybersecurity audit.\n*   Did not rely primarily on assertions or attestations by the business\u2019s management.\n\n### Certification of Completion Requirements\nAdditionally, each calendar year a business is required to perform an audit, it must submit a written certification of completion to the Agency via its website (**\u00a7 7124(a), (d)**). This certification must be completed by a member of the business\u2019s executive management team who is directly responsible for compliance, has sufficient knowledge of the audit, and has the authority to submit the certification (**\u00a7 7124(c)**).\n\nPer **\u00a7 7124(d)(4)**, this certification must include an electronically signed attestation to the following statement:\n\n> \u201cI attest that I meet the requirements of California Code of Regulations, Title 11, section 7124, subsection (c), to submit this certification. Under penalty of perjury under the laws of the state of California, I hereby declare that the information contained within and submitted with this certification is true and correct and that the business has not made any attempt to influence the auditor\u2019s decisions or assessments regarding the cybersecurity audit.\u201d\n\nThe certification must also include the name and business title of the person submitting it and the date of the certification (**\u00a7 7124(d)(5)**).",
