    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.0,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7025(d), neither the business nor the platform/technology sending the opt-out preference signal shall use, disclose, or retain any personal information collected in connection with sending or processing the signal for ANY purpose other than sending or processing the opt-out preference signal.",
    "generated_answer": "No, businesses are not permitted to use, disclose, or retain personal information collected from a consumer in connection with the sending or processing of an opt-out preference signal for any purpose other than facilitating that request.\n\nAccording to the provided regulatory context:\n\n*   **Prohibited Uses**: Per **\u00a7 7025(d)**, a business and the platform, technology, or mechanism sending the signal shall not use, disclose, or retain any personal information collected in connection with the sending or processing of the opt-out preference signal for any purpose other than sending or processing the opt-out preference signal itself.\n*   **Minimal Information**: Per **\u00a7 7025(b)(2)**, a business shall not require a consumer to provide additional information beyond what is necessary to send the signal. While a business may offer the option to provide additional information to help facilitate the request (such as identifying information for offline sales), any information provided must not be used, disclosed, or retained for any purpose other than processing the opt-out request.\n\nIn summary, the information transmitted via an opt-out preference signal is strictly limited to the function of processing the opt-out request and cannot be utilized for secondary purposes such as marketing analytics.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9316,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
