  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7025_005",
    "query": "If a business sells personal information both online and offline, can it rely on frictionless opt-out signal processing to omit its 'Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information' footer link?",
    "target_section": "7025",
    "expected_route": "HYBRID_BOTH",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": false,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7025(g)(3) and Example (A) in \u00a7 7025(g)(3), a business can only omit footer links if the opt-out preference signal FULLY effectuates the consumer's opt-out request without needing extra information. If a business sells data offline and requires additional consumer information (e.g., account login) to apply the opt-out to offline sales, the signal does not fully effectuate the request, and the business CANNOT claim the link exemption under Civil Code \u00a7 1798.135(b)(1).",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, a business that sells personal information both online and offline generally **cannot** rely on frictionless opt-out signal processing to omit the \"Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information\" link if the opt-out preference signal fails to fully effectuate the request for both channels.\n\nAccording to the regulations, the requirements are as follows:\n\n*   **Requirement for Full Effectuation**: To qualify for the exception under Civil Code section 1798.135, subdivision (b)(1) (which allows a business to omit the link), the business must ensure that the opt-out preference signal \"fully effectuates the consumer\u2019s request to opt-out of sale/sharing\" [\u00a7 7025(g)(3)].\n*   **Offline Limitations**: The regulations explicitly state that if a business sells or shares personal information offline and requires additional information from the consumer (such as logging into an account) to apply the opt-out request to those offline sales, the opt-out preference signal does not fully effectuate the request [\u00a7 7025(g)(3)].\n*   **Illustrative Example**: The provided context uses \"Business Q\" as an example of a company that collects browsing history online and sells personal information offline. Because the opt-out preference signal only applies to the online sharing of information (the browser/device level) and cannot apply to offline sales without additional information from the consumer, Business Q **cannot** fall within the exception and must provide the link [\u00a7 7025(g)(3)(A)].\n\n**Summary of Compliance**:\nIf a business processes opt-out preference signals in a frictionless manner, it may omit the \"Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information\" link **only if** the signal effectively opts the consumer out of *all* sales and sharing, including offline activities. If the business requires additional steps or information to apply the opt-out to its offline sales, it does not meet the requirements of \u00a7 7025(g)(3) and must continue to provide the link.",
