    "ground_truth_answer": "Yes. Under \u00a7 7011(e)(2)(H), a business's privacy policy must explain the consumer's right not to receive discriminatory treatment or retaliation for exercising CCPA rights. This explicitly covers applicants to educational programs, job applicants, students, employees, and independent contractors.",
    "generated_answer": "Yes, the CCPA right against retaliation for exercising privacy rights extends to job applicants, students, and independent contractors, and must be included in a business's privacy policy disclosures.\n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7011(a)(H)**, the privacy policy must include an explanation of the right not to be retaliated against for exercising privacy rights conferred by the CCPA, specifically including instances where a consumer is:\n*   An applicant to an educational program;\n*   A job applicant;\n*   A student;\n*   An employee; or\n*   An independent contractor.\n\nThis disclosure is required as part of the section of the privacy policy that explains how consumers can exercise their CCPA rights and what they can expect from that process (**\u00a7 7011(a)(3)**).",
