  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7025_007",
    "query": "What steps may a business take if an opt-out preference signal conflicts with a consumer's participation in a loyalty or financial incentive program that requires data sharing?",
    "target_section": "7025",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7025(c)(4), the business may notify the consumer that processing the signal will withdraw them from the financial incentive program and ask them to affirm their intent to withdraw. If affirmed, the signal is processed and they are withdrawn. If the consumer does not affirm withdrawal (or if the business does not ask), the business MUST still process the signal for that browser/device, but may ignore the signal regarding participation in the incentive program if the user is known.",
    "generated_answer": "When an opt-out preference signal conflicts with a consumer\u2019s participation in a financial incentive program that requires consent to the sale or sharing of personal information, a business may take the following steps under **\u00a7 7025(b)(4)**:\n\n*   **Notify the consumer**: The business may notify the consumer that processing the opt-out preference signal as a valid request would result in the consumer\u2019s withdrawal from the financial incentive program.\n*   **Request affirmation**: The business may ask the consumer to affirm that they intend to withdraw from the program.\n*   **Process based on consumer response**:\n    *   **If the consumer affirms intent to withdraw**: The business must process the request to opt-out of sale/sharing.\n    *   **If the business asks and the consumer does not affirm**: The business may ignore the opt-out preference signal regarding that consumer\u2019s participation in the financial incentive program for as long as the consumer is known to the business.\n*   **Optional processing**: If the business chooses not to ask the consumer to affirm their intent to withdraw, it must still process the opt-out preference signal as a valid request to opt-out of sale/sharing for that browser/device and any associated consumer profile.\n\n**Required Disclosures**:\nRegardless of whether the business chooses to ask for affirmation or proceed with the opt-out, it must display the status of the consumer\u2019s choice in accordance with **\u00a7 7025(c)(6)** and **\u00a7 7026(g)**.\n\n**Important Limitations**:\n*   **No \"Whitelist\" requirement**: If a consumer does not affirm their intent to withdraw, the business may place that consumer on a whitelist so it does not have to notify the consumer of the conflict again, provided the business knows it is the same consumer (**\u00a7 7025(b)(7)(D)**).\n*   **Discrimination**: Per **\u00a7 7080**, any price or service difference (such as denying loyalty program participation) must be non-discriminatory. A price or service difference is non-discriminatory only if it is reasonably related to the value of the consumer\u2019s data. If the business cannot show that the difference is reasonably related to the value of the consumer\u2019s data, the practice is considered discriminatory and prohibited under **\u00a7 7080(b)**.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8777,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 11.386,
