  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7063_002",
    "query": "What data use restrictions and security duties apply directly to authorized agents handling consumer personal data under section 7063?",
    "target_section": "7063",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7063(c)\u2013(d), an authorized agent must implement and maintain reasonable security procedures to protect consumer information. Furthermore, an authorized agent is strictly prohibited from using a consumer's personal information for any purpose other than fulfilling the consumer's requests, verification, or fraud prevention.",
    "generated_answer": "Under California privacy regulations, authorized agents are subject to specific data use restrictions and security obligations when handling consumer personal information. According to **\u00a7 7063**, the following duties apply:\n\n### Data Use Restrictions\n*   **Purpose Limitation**: An authorized agent is prohibited from using a consumer's personal information\u2014or any information collected from or about the consumer\u2014for any purpose other than the following:\n    *   Fulfilling the consumer\u2019s requests (e.g., requests to delete, correct, or know).\n    *   Verification purposes.\n    *   Fraud prevention.\n    *   (Source: **\u00a7 7063(d)**)\n\n### Security Duties\n*   **Reasonable Security**: An authorized agent is required to implement and maintain reasonable security procedures and practices to protect the consumer\u2019s information.\n    *   (Source: **\u00a7 7063(c)**)\n\n***\n\n**Compliance Note Regarding Authorization**: \nWhile not a \"use\" restriction, agents must be properly authorized to act. A business may require the agent to provide proof of signed permission from the consumer. However, a business may not require power of attorney for an agent to act on a consumer's behalf, and the agent must be exempted from the standard proof-of-permission requirements if the consumer has provided the agent with power of attorney pursuant to Probate Code sections 4121 to 4130 (**\u00a7 7063(a) and (b)**).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8805,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 11.419,
