    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7026(j), while a business may generally require an authorized agent to present written permission signed by the consumer, this requirement does NOT apply to opt-out requests submitted automatically via an opt-out preference signal.",
    "generated_answer": "No, an authorized agent is not always required to provide signed written permission from the consumer. \n\nAccording to the provided regulatory context:\n\n*   **Standard Rule**: Generally, a consumer may use an authorized agent to submit requests to opt-out of sale/sharing (\u00a7 7026(j)), opt-out of Automated Decision-Making Technology (ADMT) (\u00a7 7221(j)), or limit the use and disclosure of sensitive personal information (\u00a7 7027(j)) if the consumer provides the agent with written permission signed by the consumer. A business may deny such a request if the agent does not provide this signed permission.\n*   **Exception for Opt-Out Preference Signals**: The requirement to obtain and provide written permission from the consumer **does not apply** to requests made via an opt-out preference signal (\u00a7 7026(j)).\n*   **Power of Attorney Exception**: While not specifically tied to the opt-out preference signal, under \u00a7 7063(b), the requirement to provide signed permission does not apply when a consumer has provided the authorized agent with power of attorney pursuant to Probate Code sections 4121 to 4130. A business cannot require a power of attorney in order for a consumer to use an authorized agent to act on their behalf (\u00a7 7063(b)).",
