    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7221(e)\u2013(f), a business must NOT require a consumer to create an account or submit a verifiable consumer request to opt-out of ADMT. The business may ask only for minimal information necessary to direct the opt-out, and if it can comply with the request without additional information, it must do so immediately.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, a business is **not** permitted to require identity verification or the creation of an account for a request to opt-out of Automated Decisionmaking Technology (ADMT).\n\nThe specific requirements are as follows:\n\n*   **No Verification Required**: Under **\u00a7 7060(b)** and **\u00a7 7221(f)**, a business shall not require a consumer to verify their identity to make a request to opt-out of ADMT.\n*   **No Account Creation Required**: Under **\u00a7 7221(e)**, a business must not require a consumer to create an account to submit a request to opt-out of ADMT.\n*   **Information Collection Limits**: While a business may ask a consumer for information necessary to complete the request (such as information to identify the consumer), it must not be burdensome. Under **\u00a7 7221(f)**, to the extent that a business can comply with the request without additional information, it must do so.\n*   **Prohibition on Burdensome Requests**: Per **\u00a7 7060(b)**, businesses must not impose burdensome requirements, such as requiring a consumer to take a picture of themselves with their driver\u2019s license.\n\n**Note on Fraud**: While verification is not permitted, **\u00a7 7221(g)** provides that if a business has a \"good-faith, reasonable, and documented belief that a request to opt-out of ADMT is fraudulent,\" it may deny the request, provided it informs the requester of the reason for the denial.",
