    "key_term_recall": 0.25,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7062(g), if a business has no reasonable method to verify consumers, it must explain why in its privacy policy. The business MUST evaluate and document whether a reasonable verification method can be established at least once every 12 months, in connection with its annual privacy policy update.",
    "generated_answer": "If a business determines that it has no reasonable method to verify the identity of any consumer for the purposes of CCPA/CPRA requests, it must evaluate and document whether a reasonable method can be established at least once every 12 months (\u00a7 7062(g)).\n\nThis evaluation must be conducted in connection with the requirement to update the business's privacy policy as set forth in Civil Code section 1798.130, subdivision (a)(5) (\u00a7 7062(g)).\n\nAdditionally, when a business has no reasonable verification method, it is required to explain this in its privacy policy (\u00a7 7062(g)).",
