  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7221_002",
    "query": "Under what narrow conditions can a business deny an ADMT opt-out request for employee hiring or work allocation decisions without providing a human appeal option?",
    "target_section": "7221",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.25,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7221(b)(2)\u2013(3), opt-out exceptions apply to hiring/admission or work allocation/compensation decisions ONLY IF: (1) the ADMT is used solely to assess the consumer's ability to perform at work/school or solely for work allocation/compensation; AND (2) the ADMT demonstrably works for the business's purpose and does not unlawfully discriminate based on protected characteristics.",
    "generated_answer": "Under the provided regulatory framework, a business is not required to provide consumers the ability to opt-out of the use of Automated Decisionmaking Technology (ADMT) to make a significant decision\u2014specifically for hiring or work allocation\u2014if the conditions set forth in **\u00a7 7221(b)(2)** or **\u00a7 7221(b)(3)** are met.\n\n### 1. Hiring, Admission, or Acceptance Decisions\nA business may deny an opt-out request for ADMT used in admission, acceptance, or hiring decisions (as defined in **\u00a7 7001(ddd)(3)(A)** and **(ddd)(4)(A)**) without a human appeal process if the following are true:\n*   The business uses the ADMT **solely** for the purpose of assessing the consumer\u2019s ability to perform at work or in an educational program to determine whether to admit, accept, or hire them (**\u00a7 7221(b)(2)(A)**); and\n*   The ADMT is effective for the business\u2019s intended purpose and does not unlawfully discriminate based upon protected characteristics (**\u00a7 7221(b)(2)(B)**).\n\n### 2. Allocation or Assignment of Work and Compensation\nA business may deny an opt-out request for ADMT used for the allocation or assignment of work and compensation (as defined in **\u00a7 7001(ddd)(4)(B)**) without a human appeal process if the following are true:\n*   The business uses the ADMT **solely** for the allocation or assignment of work or compensation (**\u00a7 7221(b)(3)(A)**); and\n*   The ADMT is effective for the business\u2019s intended purpose and does not unlawfully discriminate based upon protected characteristics (**\u00a7 7221(b)(3)(B)**).\n\n***\n\n### Additional Compliance Requirements\nIf a business relies on these specific exceptions to deny an opt-out request, it must fulfill the following notification obligations:\n*   **Pre-use Notice**: The business must provide a Pre-use Notice to the consumer (**\u00a7 7220(a)**).\n*   **Exception Identification**: In the Pre-use Notice, the business must clearly identify the specific exception from **\u00a7 7221(b)** upon which it is relying (**\u00a7 7220(c)(2)(B)**).\n\n*Note: While these subsections provide an exception to the requirement to offer an opt-out, they do not waive the general prohibition against retaliating against consumers for exercising their CCPA rights (**\u00a7 7221(l)**).*",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8604,
    "llm_judge_score": null,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": null,
    "execution_time_sec": 9.419,
